2. ADR Identification and Analysis

A. Suspected drug: Amoxicillin

Amoxicillin is the primary suspected drug because the patient developed a skin rash, itching, nausea, and dizziness after starting treatment.

Amoxicillin can cause gastrointestinal adverse effects, including nausea, and can also cause hypersensitivity reactions that manifest as skin rash and itching. Dizziness may also occur, although it is nonspecific.

The timing is compatible with a possible drug-related reaction. However, the available information is insufficient to establish that amoxicillin definitely caused all the reported symptoms.

B. Assessment of paracetamol

Paracetamol is another medication in the patient's history. Although it can occasionally cause skin reactions and other adverse effects, the screenshot does not specify when the patient last took it or whether the symptoms occurred in relation to its use.

Therefore, paracetamol cannot be completely excluded as a possible contributor. Additional information is required to assess its role.

3. Timeline Assessment
